AUDIO: 'on a single team, a single database, and a single project at a time. We\'re still blazing down the path with Directus, uh but in the meantime we\'ve actually identified a new problem space. Rich? &gt;&gt; So what we notice is larger enterprises,'

[01:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'especially those with more complex data problems, have a lot of new problems to solve recently. And those new problems require new solutions. Uh especially a more tailored solution. Cuz what we\'ve'

[01:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seen is that big companies oftentimes have multiple teams, obviously so. Uh all of which have multiple different projects that they\'re managing. And all of those projects also need to be able to be independently owned by those'

[01:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'teams, but still be within the organization for data access and governance control. So what we\'ve seen is sort of a pendulum swing back into data siloing becoming more and more of'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an issue now that there\'s so many more different services and more teams building different things. Of course, within an organization that data volume needs to be sustained without things'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slowing down organization-wide, but that really creates a new bigger data governance and visibility problem. And now with AI coming into the mix, we\'re seeing some new issues as well.'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ben? &gt;&gt; Yeah, well, I mean, all all of the business users want to build on that enterprise data, you know, core core set, not just the technical engineers. But, the problem is the business users, the AI agents, pretty much everybody can\'t'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get access to that core data. So, their ideas, their innovation ends up stalling out before it can even really get started. &gt;&gt; So, to solve those new problems, we\'ve actually added a whole new team and'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spent significant time building a new product in stealth, a product we call Monospace. So, Ben, what is Monospace? &gt;&gt; Well, in in addition to being our formal company name, it\'s also the unified back-end platform that we\'ve been'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working on that will power the entire organization. So, when that organization needs to build for their business users, AI agents, the front-end engineers, the back-end engineers, basically everybody can have this purpose-built enterprise'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'platform that has a couple really important differences. One is multi-tenancy. So, multiple projects or workspaces can be powered by a single instance.'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can take multiple data sources. So, all that siloed disparate data can get connected in, whether it\'s new or old. And then we also are still going to support all the different types of builders, so technical, non-technical agents, etc. And then we take all of'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that and wrap it in enterprise governance at scale. So, you can imagine the RBA and everything else that\'s needed to keep things tightly locked down. So, in simple terms, Monospace is what sits'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between your enterprise data and everybody who needs it to start building. Uh, but Rich, maybe you can dive into what that means in practice. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So, what that boils down to is where before we were hyper-focused on solving the content management for a'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single problem, or a single project rather, well, I guess both. Um, this new product really focuses on the layer above it. It\'s like, how do we orchestrate all the data coming in from all these desperate sources, and how do we manage the access of that data going'

